Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedjulian.shoddy — reckoner seed: julian.shoddy, converting at
Rem the bridge
Rem
Rem The calendar at the prompt: building a date, writing it out and
Rem reading it back, leap years and month lengths, the two epochs
Rem (serial days from 1970-01-01 and the astronomical Julian day
Rem number), date arithmetic and the weekday. These words lived in
Rem seedfin while fin.shoddy carried the calendar as a stated stand-in;
Rem when the calendar moved to julian.shoddy the words moved with it,
Rem renamed JUL* to say whose they are. The day-count BASES and the
Rem bond words stayed in seedfin, exactly as FinDays360 stayed in fin.
Rem
Rem A DATE IS A DICT, the same LIST-of-PAIR shape seeddict.shoddy
Rem established and seedfin's records use:
Rem { ("yr" n) ("mon" n) ("day" n) }, built by JULDATE and read back by
Rem JULDATESHOW as "2025-01-31". JULDATEOF parses that same spelling,
Rem so a date can be typed either way round and a tape reads as prose.
Rem The keys are the same three seedfin's dates always carried, so a
Rem date built here flows straight into FINDAYS360 and the bond words.
Rem
Rem WHY THE DATE IS VALIDATED AT ALL. JulSerial is pure integer
Rem arithmetic over year/month/day and has no opinion about whether the
Rem date exists: the raw constructor serializes a 30th of February
Rem perfectly happily to the 2nd of March. julian.shoddy's own checked
Rem builder aborts, and NOTHING HERE MAY ABORT — so JULDATE asks
Rem JulTryOf first and turns its Err into a refusal, and every word
Rem that takes a date checks it again on the way in, since a dict can
Rem also arrive hand-built.
Rem
Rem THE SAME REASONING SETS THE OTHER GUARDS. JulFromSerial aborts on a
Rem fractional serial — the guard that catches a Julian day number
Rem handed to the wrong epoch — so JULFROMSERIAL asks the same question
Rem first and refuses instead. The offsets to JULADDDAYS and
Rem JULADDMONTHS must be whole for the same reason, and negative
Rem offsets are ordinary questions that must not be refused: days back,
Rem months back, and a serial before 1970 all run both ways.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "julian.shoddy"
Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- shared readers -----------------------------------------------------

Rem A whole number that may be NEGATIVE, which RckWholeArg is not: its
Rem whole numbers start at zero, and every offset in a calendar runs
Rem both ways.
Def JsStepArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            JsStepWhole(word, x)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a NUMBER", c), 0)

Def JsStepWhole(word As String, x As Number) As Result
    If x <> Floor(x) Then
        Err(word & " needs a whole number, got " & Str(x), 0)
    Else
        Ok(x)

Def JsListNum(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case RckListArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(xs)
            JsNumsAt(word, xs, { })
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Def JsNumsAt(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, acc As List Of Number) As Result
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        Ok(acc)
    Else
        JsNumsNext(word, xs, acc, First(xs))

Def JsNumsNext(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, acc As List Of Number, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            JsNumsAt(word, Rest(xs), Append(acc, x))
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a LIST of NUMBERs", c), 0)

Def JsEntry(k As String, v As Cell) As Cell
    CutPair(Pair(CutStr(k), v))

Rem ---- the date dict, in and out ------------------------------------------

Def JsDateCell(d As JulDate) As Cell
    CutList({ JsEntry("yr", CutNum(JulYear(d))), JsEntry("mon", CutNum(JulMonth(d))), JsEntry("day", CutNum(JulDay(d))) })

Def JsDateWhy(word As String) As String
    word & ": NOT A DATE — USE JULDATE, OR JULDATEOF ON " & RckQuoteMark() & "YYYY-MM-DD" & RckQuoteMark()

Def JsDateOf(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case RckListArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(fields)
            JsDateFields(word, fields)
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Def JsDateFields(word As String, fields As List Of Cell) As Result
    If Length(fields) <> 3 Then
        Err(JsDateWhy(word), 0)
    Else
        JsDateNums(word, Map(fields, JsPairVal))

Def JsPairVal(entry As Cell) As Cell
    Select Case entry
        Case CutPair(p)
            Snd(p)
        Case Else
            entry

Def JsDateNums(word As String, vals As List Of Cell) As Result
    Select Case JsListNum(word, CutList(vals))
        Case Ok(ns)
            JsDateMade(word, Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), Nth(ns, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(JsDateWhy(word), 0)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Rem The one calendar check, used by JULDATE on the way in and by every
Rem date-taking word on a dict that may have been built by hand. The
Rem rules are julian's own — JulTryOf — with the month and day named
Rem separately so a refusal says which one is wrong and what the real
Rem month length was.
Def JsDateMade(word As String, y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As Result
    If y <> Floor(y) Or m <> Floor(m) Or d <> Floor(d) Then
        Err(word & ": A YEAR, MONTH AND DAY MUST ALL BE WHOLE NUMBERS", 0)
    Else
        JsDateReal(word, y, m, d)

Def JsDateReal(word As String, y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As Result
    If m < 1 Or m > 12 Then
        Err(word & ": THERE IS NO MONTH " & Str(m), 0)
    Else
        JsDateDay(word, y, m, d)

Def JsDateDay(word As String, y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As Result
    If d < 1 Or d > JulDaysInMonth(y, m) Then
        Err(word & ": " & Str(m) & "/" & Str(y) & " HAS " & Str(JulDaysInMonth(y, m)) & " DAYS, SO THERE IS NO DAY " & Str(d), 0)
    Else
        Ok(JulDate(y, m, d))

Rem ---- the words -----------------------------------------------------------

Def JsSeedDates(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "JULDATE", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsDate(vs)), "( y m d -- date )", "A date as a dict: yr, mon, day. Refuses anything the calendar does not have, such as the 30th of February.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "JULDATESHOW", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsDateshow(vs)), "( date -- str )", "A date written out as " & RckQuoteMark() & "YYYY-MM-DD" & RckQuoteMark() & ".")
    Let s3 = RckReg(s2, "JULDATEOF", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsFromtext(vs)), "( str -- date )", "The other way: " & RckQuoteMark() & "2025-01-31" & RckQuoteMark() & " read back into a date.")
    Let s4 = RckReg(s3, "JULISLEAP", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsIsleap(vs)), "( y -- flag )", "Whether a year is a leap year, century rules included.")
    Let s5 = RckReg(s4, "JULDAYSINMONTH", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsDaysinmonth(vs)), "( y m -- n )", "How many days that month has in that year.")
    Let s6 = RckReg(s5, "JULSERIAL", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsSerial(vs)), "( date -- n )", "A date as a day number, counting from 1970-01-01 — the form to subtract two dates with.")
    Let s7 = RckReg(s6, "JULFROMSERIAL", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsFromserial(vs)), "( n -- date )", "A day number back into a date. Refuses a fraction: a number ending in .5 is usually a Julian day number on the wrong epoch.")
    Let s8 = RckReg(s7, "JULDAYNUMBER", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsDaynumber(vs)), "( date -- jd )", "The astronomical Julian day number at midnight UT — always ends in .5, and it is what the geo sun words take.")
    Let s9 = RckReg(s8, "JULADDDAYS", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsAdddays(vs)), "( date days -- date )", "The date that many days later; a negative count goes back.")
    Let s10 = RckReg(s9, "JULADDMONTHS", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsAddmonths(vs)), "( date months -- date )", "That many months later, clamped to month end: one month after 31 January is 28 February.")
    Let s11 = RckReg(s10, "JULDIFF", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsDiff(vs)), "( a b -- days )", "Days from a to b, signed: negative when b falls before a.")
    RckReg(s11, "JULWEEKDAY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => JsWeekday(vs)), "( date -- n )", "The day of the week, 1..7 with Monday 1 — the ISO convention.")

Def JsDate(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case JsListNum("JULDATE", CutList(vs))
        Case Ok(ns)
            JsDateAt(Nth(ns, 1), Nth(ns, 2), Nth(ns, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULDATE did not answer")

Def JsDateAt(y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As RckWork
    Select Case JsDateMade("JULDATE", y, m, d)
        Case Ok(dt)
            RckVals({ JsDateCell(dt) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULDATE did not answer")

Rem The one shape most of the calendar words have: one date in, one
Rem cell out, with the date validated on the way through.
Def JsOnDate(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ JulDate -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    Select Case JsDateOf(word, c)
        Case Ok(dt)
            RckVals({ Call(f, dt) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def JsDateshow(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsOnDate("JULDATESHOW", First(vs), Fn(d) => CutStr(JsShown(d)))

Def JsShown(d As JulDate) As String
    PadZero(JulYear(d), 4) & "-" & PadZero(JulMonth(d), 2) & "-" & PadZero(JulDay(d), 2)

Def JsSerial(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsOnDate("JULSERIAL", First(vs), Fn(d) => CutNum(JulSerial(d)))

Def JsDaynumber(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsOnDate("JULDAYNUMBER", First(vs), Fn(d) => CutNum(JulDayNumber(d)))

Def JsWeekday(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsOnDate("JULWEEKDAY", First(vs), Fn(d) => CutNum(JulWeekday(d)))

Rem "YYYY-MM-DD" and nothing else: three fields, each all digits, and
Rem then the same calendar check JULDATE makes. A lax parse here would
Rem put the wrong date on the stack rather than refuse, which is the
Rem whole failure these seeds exist to prevent.
Def JsFromtext(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("JULDATEOF", First(vs))
        Case Ok(s)
            JsFromtextParts(s, Split(s, "-"))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULDATEOF did not answer")

Def JsFromtextParts(s As String, parts As List Of String) As RckWork
    If Length(parts) <> 3 Or Not All(parts, JsAllDigits) Then
        RckWorkBad("JULDATEOF: " & RckQuoteMark() & s & RckQuoteMark() & " IS NOT A DATE — IT WANTS YYYY-MM-DD")
    Else
        JsFromtextAt(Val(Nth(parts, 1)), Val(Nth(parts, 2)), Val(Nth(parts, 3)))

Def JsAllDigits(s As String) As Boolean
    s <> "" And All(Map(Range(1, Len(s)), Fn(k) => Mid(s, k, 1)), Fn(c) => Instr("0123456789", c) > 0)

Def JsFromtextAt(y As Number, m As Number, d As Number) As RckWork
    Select Case JsDateMade("JULDATEOF", y, m, d)
        Case Ok(dt)
            RckVals({ JsDateCell(dt) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULDATEOF did not answer")

Def JsIsleap(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case JsStepArg("JULISLEAP", First(vs))
        Case Ok(y)
            RckVals({ CutBool(JulIsLeap(y)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULISLEAP did not answer")

Def JsDaysinmonth(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(JsStepArg("JULDAYSINMONTH", Nth(vs, 1)), JsStepArg("JULDAYSINMONTH", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(y), Ok(m))
            JsDaysinmonthAt(y, m)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULDAYSINMONTH did not answer")

Def JsDaysinmonthAt(y As Number, m As Number) As RckWork
    If m < 1 Or m > 12 Then
        RckWorkBad("JULDAYSINMONTH: THERE IS NO MONTH " & Str(m))
    Else
        RckVals({ CutNum(JulDaysInMonth(y, m)) })

Rem The same refusal julian's own guard makes as an abort, asked here
Rem first: a fractional serial is usually a Julian day number on the
Rem wrong epoch, and the answer is a refusal that says so, not a date
Rem five thousand years wide of the mark and not a dead session.
Def JsFromserial(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case JsStepArg("JULFROMSERIAL", First(vs))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ JsDateCell(JulFromSerial(n)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("JULFROMSERIAL did not answer")

Def JsAdddays(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsDateAndWhole("JULADDDAYS", vs, Fn(d, n) => JsDateCell(JulAddDays(d, n)))

Def JsAddmonths(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsDateAndWhole("JULADDMONTHS", vs, Fn(d, n) => JsDateCell(JulAddMonths(d, n)))

Rem A fractional step would run through Fix and land somewhere nobody
Rem asked for, so both offsets have to be whole.
Def JsDateAndWhole(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ JulDate Number -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    Select Case JsDateOf(word, Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(d)
            JsDateAndWholeAt(word, d, Nth(vs, 2), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def JsDateAndWholeAt(word As String, d As JulDate, c As Cell, f As [ JulDate Number -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    Select Case JsStepArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ Call(f, d, n) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def JsDiff(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    JsOnTwoDates("JULDIFF", vs, Fn(a, b) => RckVals({ CutNum(JulDiff(a, b)) }))

Def JsOnTwoDates(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ JulDate JulDate -- RckWork ]) As RckWork
    Select Case JsDateOf(word, Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(a)
            JsOnTwoDatesAt(word, a, Nth(vs, 2), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def JsOnTwoDatesAt(word As String, a As JulDate, c As Cell, f As [ JulDate JulDate -- RckWork ]) As RckWork
    Select Case JsDateOf(word, c)
        Case Ok(b)
            Call(f, a, b)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- the seed -----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedJulian(st As RckState) As RckState
    JsSeedDates(RckSeeding(st, "seed-julian"))
